# Appendix H — Troubleshooting & Fixing Broken Games

Every game breaks while you build it. This appendix is the repair shelf: the two
ways a game goes wrong, how to *read* the console when it does, how to catch a
crash so the rest of your game keeps running, and the one true undo — loading
your last good save. Every claim here is something the console will confirm at
the prompt, so it does.

## There are only two kinds of broken

A game breaks in one of exactly two ways, and telling them apart is half the
repair:

1. **It crashes** — the console stops with a red `error:` line and refuses to go
   on. This is the *loud* break, and it is the friendly one: the message names
   what went wrong.
2. **It runs but is wrong** — nothing crashes, yet the hero walks through walls
   or the screen stays black. This is the *quiet* break, and it is the hard one,
   because the console thinks everything is fine.

The rest of this appendix is one section per kind, then the undo that fixes
either.

## The loud break: read the error, it names the culprit

The most common crash is a **typo in a verb name**. You meant `spawn`, you typed
`spawm`, and the console has never heard of `spawm`. Left alone it stops the
whole program — but you can *catch* the crash with `guard` and keep running:

```motoi
(display (guard (e (#t 'caught-a-typo))
           (spawm 'goblin)))
(newline)
```

```
caught-a-typo
```

The verb `spawm` does not exist, so calling it raised an *unbound symbol* error.
Uncaught, that error prints `error: unbound symbol: spawm` and stops the console
— which is exactly the message you want, because it hands you the misspelled name
to fix. Here `guard` caught it instead and answered `caught-a-typo`, so a bad
enemy did not take down the whole game. **Unbound symbol** is the error you will
see most; it almost always means *check the spelling of that verb*.

## Raise your own error, then read it back

You do not have to wait for the console to find trouble — you can `raise` your
own error the moment your game logic goes impossible, and attach the numbers that
prove it. `error` takes a message and any number of *irritants* (the values that
were wrong), and `guard` hands you the whole thing to inspect:

```motoi
(define (take-damage hp amount)
  (if (> amount hp)
      (error \"hp would go negative\" hp amount)
      (- hp amount)))
(display (guard (e (#t (list (error-object-message e)
                             (error-object-irritants e))))
           (take-damage 3 10)))
(newline)
```

```
(hp would go negative (3 10))
```

The hero had `3` hp and took `10` damage, which should never drop health below
zero — so `take-damage` raised an error carrying the message *and* the two
numbers. `error-object-message` read back the words; `error-object-irritants`
read back `(3 10)`, the exact values that broke the rule. This is how you make a
game that *tells you* what went wrong instead of silently producing a hero with
`-7` health three screens later.

## The quiet break: the console is forgiving, which hides mistakes

The harder bug makes no noise. Motoi is deliberately gentle with out-of-range
numbers — it clips and shrugs rather than crashing — and that mercy can *hide* a
mistake. Draw a pixel far off the `80`×`80` screen and read it back:

```motoi
(pset 200 50 12)
(display (pget 200 50))
(newline)
```

```
0
```

Nothing crashed — `pset` saw that `(200, 50)` is off the screen and quietly
clipped it, drawing nothing. Reading that same off-screen spot with `pget`
returns `0`, the empty color, as if you never tried. If your sprite is
mysteriously invisible, this is why: your coordinates left the `0`–`79` range and
the console said nothing. When a game *runs* but looks wrong, suspect the quiet
break — print your coordinates, your colors, your state — and check them against
the ranges the console actually keeps.

## The one true undo: load your last good save

There is no separate 'broken-games' folder and no automatic undo (Appendix F).
The real undo is the one you make yourself: **save when the game is good, and
load that save when you break it.** Watch it rescue a pixel:

```motoi
(pset 5 5 11)
(save-cart \"level-1-good\")
(pset 5 5 3)
(load-cart \"level-1-good\")
(display (pget 5 5))
(newline)
```

```
11
```

You lit `(5, 5)` to color `11` and saved that as `level-1-good`. Then you 'broke'
it — repainted the pixel to `3`. `load-cart` pulled the good snapshot back and
`(5, 5)` is `11` again: the break is gone. A broken cart is not special; it saves
into `carts/` like any other, so the discipline is to **save good states under
names you trust** and load one back the moment things go wrong. That is your undo,
your checkpoint, and your safety net, all in one verb.

> **Tip**
>
> Build a *repair habit*: when something breaks, ask the two questions in order.
> First, *did it crash?* — if yes, read the `error:` line; it names the verb or
> the value. Second, *if it did not crash, what did I expect versus what
> happened?* — print the numbers and compare them to the ranges (`0`–`79` for the
> screen, `0`–`15` for colors). Loud breaks are read; quiet breaks are printed.

> **Warning**
>
> The console's forgiveness is real and it is a trap. Off-screen `pset` clips,
> out-of-range `pget` returns `0`, and a bad color number is not always refused —
> so *no crash does not mean no bug*. When a game runs but plays wrong, do not
> wait for an error that will never come: print your own values and check them, or
> raise your own `error` the moment a rule is violated, as the health example did.

